<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · war</p><h1 class="enc-title">French and Indian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1754">1754</a>–<a href="/years/1763">1763</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Fourth Intercolonial War</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The French and Indian War was a conflict in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> from 1754 to 1763 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and their Indigenous allies, generally seen as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>. It grew from a dispute over the Forks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a> and began in May 1754, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> militia under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> ambushed a French patrol at Jumonville Glen. After early defeats, among them the Braddock Expedition at the Monongahela in 1755, William Pitt increased British forces, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> in 1759 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> the following year. From 1755 thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadians">Acadians</a> were deported. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1763 France ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and its claims east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a> to Britain.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>East of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> was largely claimed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The French population numbered about 75,000, heavily concentrated along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St. Lawrence</a> valley, and French fur traders dealt with local peoples and often married Indigenous women. British settlers, about 1.5 million along the Atlantic coast, outnumbered the French twenty to one, and many older colonies claimed land arbitrarily far to the west.</p><p>In June 1747 the Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>, Roland-Michel Barrin de La Galissonière, ordered Pierre-Joseph Céloron to lead an expedition through the Ohio Country, where British traders such as George Croghan were gaining influence. Céloron buried lead plates engraved with the French claim. The Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle of 1748 left the colonial claims to a commission that reached no decision, and in 1749 the British government gave land to the Ohio Company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> also claimed the territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>After Washington's force of 52 ambushed 40 Canadiens on 28 May 1754, killing their commander Joseph Coulon de Jumonville, he built Fort Necessity, where he surrendered after an attack on 3 July and negotiated a withdrawal under arms. In June 1755 Braddock led about 1,500 regulars and militia towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-duquesne">Fort Duquesne</a>, was ambushed and killed, and approximately 1,000 British soldiers were killed or injured. Washington led the remaining 500 back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> formally declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 17 May 1756, which widened the conflict into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. After the failures of 1757, William Pitt dominated military planning, and in 1758 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-duquesne">Fort Duquesne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisbourg">Louisbourg</a> fell to British forces. French resources were thinned by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>, a poor harvest in 1757 and the alleged corruption of the intendant François Bigot. Governor Vaudreuil negotiated a capitulation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> with General Amherst in September 1760, allowing residents who stayed to keep their Catholic worship and property.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> ceded its Canadian possessions by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1763 and gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-fontainebleau-1762">Treaty of Fontainebleau</a>. Britain gained colonies with about 80,000 mainly French-speaking Roman Catholic residents. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadians">Acadians</a> were resettled across the British provinces, and many went to France or to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>, where their descendants are linked to the Cajun population. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-proclamation-of-1763">Royal Proclamation of 1763</a>, issued on 7 October, reserved lands west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appalachian-mountains">Appalachian Mountains</a> for the Indian population. General Amherst's policies on the western frontier disturbed many Native peoples and contributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontiac">Pontiac</a>'s War of 1763.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “French and Indian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Haudenosaunee backed the British in the <b>French and Indian War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/haudenosaunee">Haudenosaunee · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The song is said to have been first sung by British officers to mock the colonial &quot;Yankees&quot; who served beside them in the <b>French and Indian War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/yankee-doodle">Yankee Doodle</a></li><li><blockquote>It was the only surrender of his military career and an opening clash of the <b>French and Indian War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-3">3 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Anglo-Cherokee War, fought during the <b>French and Indian War</b>, Cherokee warriors under Seroweh defeated a British and colonial force under Archibald Montgomerie at Etchoe Pass, near present-day Otto in Macon County, North Carolina.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-27">27 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
